---
title: Rust and Cargo
description: Create applications, libraries, and workspaces while keeping Cargo in charge of builds and dependencies.
section: Guides
order: 20
updated: '2026-10-09'
---

Choose Rust, then an application, library, or workspace. Editions 2024 and 2021 are offered, with 2024 first. The starter has a dependency-free library, a correctness test, and an application when requested.

## First run in Neovim

After [loading Rivet](/docs/start-here/), make and open a folder you choose:

```sh
mkdir compute_demo
cd compute_demo
nvim
```

1. Run `:RivetNew`, choose **New choices** if a profile menu appears, then select `rust`, **Current folder**, and `compute_demo`.
2. Choose Git and comment-header preferences. Select **Cargo workspace with an application and library**, then edition **2024**. Review and apply with `c`.
3. Open `crates/compute_demo/src/main.rs`. Run `:RivetTask run` and select Debug; the application prints `42`.
4. Run `:RivetTask test`. The library's correctness test should pass. Cargo may also report binary/doc-test suites containing zero tests.
5. Run `:RivetFile`, enter `feature`, and add `mod feature;` to the appropriate `main.rs` or `lib.rs`. Save and run `:RivetTask check` to compile the change.

To try error navigation, temporarily change the call to `add` in `main.rs` to `missing`, save, and run Check. Use `:copen` to jump to the compiler diagnostic, correct the name, then `:RivetTask repeat`.

The equivalent optional CLI workflow is:

```sh
rivet new compute_demo --language rust --kind workspace --edition 2024
rivet test --root compute_demo
rivet run --root compute_demo
```

The workspace contains `crates/compute_demo`. Tests check `add(20, 22) == 42` and `add(-1, 1) == 0`; the executable prints `42`.

## Cargo owns the workflow

| Task | Cargo operation |
| --- | --- |
| Configure | `metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps` |
| Check / Build / Test | Native command across workspace members |
| Run | Selected package and binary, with arguments after `--` |
| Format | `fmt --all` |
| Lint | `clippy` with warnings denied |
| Package | `package --workspace --no-verify` (does not publish) |

Debug and Release map to Cargo's ordinary profiles. `jobs` controls Cargo build concurrency. Install rustfmt/Clippy components explicitly if your toolchain lacks them.

Binary discovery comes from `cargo metadata`, including workspaces. The picker shows package and binary together; an explicit CLI selector can use `package::binary`. Build diagnostics use Cargo JSON compiler messages and primary spans for quickfix. [Cargo's editor integration interfaces](https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/external-tools.html).

## Add and edit code

`:RivetTarget` adds an application under `src/bin/` (inside the generated member for a workspace). Cargo discovers that binary natively. `:RivetFile` adds a `.rs` module file; declare it with `mod feature;` or `pub mod feature;` in the appropriate parent module when you want it compiled. Rivet does not guess your Rust visibility or module hierarchy.

Use `cargo add`, `cargo remove`, and workspace manifests for dependency and member changes. Existing `Cargo.toml` projects are discovered without creating `.rivet.json`. Rivet preserves their dependencies, features, build scripts, and lockfiles.

For compiler problems, open `:copen`, fix the selected span, and `:RivetTask repeat`. Local task settings and custom profiler commands are described in [Task options](/docs/task-options/).
